{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 5, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Crusader ruler who played a significant role during the late 12th and early 13th centuries. He was born around 1140 to Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His lineage connected him to the powerful noble families of the Crusader states. Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1187 a year marked by the fall of Jerusalem to Saladin during the Battle of Hattin.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n**Early Life and Succession**\nRaymond III spent much of his youth in the Holy Land however his upbringing was influenced by European customs. Unlike his father he spoke and wrote French instead of Arabic. His education included military training and exposure to chivalric culture. When Raymond II died in 1187 Raymond III inherited the County of Tripoli. He was married to Eschiva of Bures who was the daughter of Hugh II of Bures. Despite his youth and inexperience he quickly took control of the county during a turbulent period in the Crusader states.\n\nFollowing Saladin’s victory at Hattin and the capture of Jerusalem Raymond III was among the few Crusader leaders who maintained resistance. He held Tripoli one of the last major strongholds in the Levant. His county had strong fortifications and a well-trained army which made it a critical bastion against Muslim expansion.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n**Political and Military Role**\nRaymond III was a key figure in the Third Crusade which began in 1189. He joined forces with Richard the Lionheart King of England and Philip II of France though his leadership was often overshadowed by these more prominent figures. He participated in the Siege of Acre in 1191 where Crusader forces recaptured the city a major strategic victory. His military contributions were crucial in maintaining Crusader control in the region.\n\nAfter the Third Crusade Raymond III focused on securing his own borders and stabilizing Tripoli. He faced internal challenges from rival nobles and external threats from Muslim forces. His rule was marked by a mix of diplomacy and warfare. He negotiated with local Muslim rulers at times and engaged in military campaigns when necessary. One notable conflict involved the city of Tortosa where Raymond III laid siege to it in 1199 as part of efforts to expand his influence along the coast.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n**Later Years and Legacy**\nIn his later years Raymond III’s health declined and he became less active in military affairs. He died in 1197 though some sources suggest his death occurred around 1213 the exact date remains uncertain. His death marked the end of the direct line of the Counts of Tripoli founded by Raymond of Toulouse.\n\nRaymond III’s legacy lies in his resilience during a period of severe crisis for the Crusader states. He defended Tripoli against overwhelming odds and contributed to the Third Crusade’s successes. His rule helped preserve the County of Tripoli as a Crusader stronghold for several decades. The county eventually fell to the Mamluks in 1289 but Raymond III’s leadership during his reign was pivotal in its survival through the most dangerous years of the Crusades.\n\nRaymond III’s story is a testament to the challenges faced by Crusader nobles who balanced European traditions with the realities of life in the Near East. His life reflects the broader struggles of the Crusader states to maintain their presence in the Holy Land against formidable opponents.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.08366527408361435}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.0001662831346038729}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.005333361681550741}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.24919773638248444}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00035089056473225355}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.01817992888391018}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.4510277509689331}, {"token": " a", "l